!! Questions

* What is a Shultz Hour?
** A weekly hour set aside, with the door closed and no interruptions, to sit alone with a pad and pen and think about the bigger picture.
* Who was [[George Shultz]]?
** Secretary of State in the 1980s. He took this hour every week because otherwise the day-to-day pulled him away from the larger questions of the national interest.

!! The argument

Leonhardt's point: good work in any field needs time to consider the big questions, and a culture that prizes being frantically busy squeezes that out. He had just started doing it himself: one hour a week with no meetings, calls or email. He cites Amos Tversky's line about being "a little underemployed" as support.

The quote it cites: [[The secret to doing good research is always to be a little underemployed. You waste years by not being able to waste hours.]]

